title: Elections beyond Poland

@western_economic_isolation
# prose-audit: Grade A — The isolation is now shown at the scale it is felt: a cohesion payment that takes five months instead of six weeks, a spread over Czech borrowing nobody can explain economically, a voivodeship whose calls are no longer returned. Repair, shield and defiance each end somewhere specific, and the shield branch is honest that it treats a symptom.
title: Western institutions price in Polish isolation
subtitle: Cold EU and US relations now reach investment, orders and jobs
face-image: img/poland/events/foreign-ministry.webp
new-page: true
tags: poland_event, poland_event_calendar
priority: 23
order: 5
max-visits: 1
view-if: continuous_campaign = 1 and year >= 2023 and western_economic_access < 34 and western_economic_shock_done = 0
call: poland_normalize
on-arrival: {!
Q.western_economic_shock_done = 1;
const accessGap = Math.max(0, 34 - Number(Q.western_economic_access));
const drag = Math.round((0.5 + accessGap * 0.04) * 10) / 10;
const jobs = Math.round((0.2 + accessGap * 0.025) * 10) / 10;
Q.western_economic_drag = drag;
Q.economic_growth -= drag;
Q.unemployment += jobs;
Q.foreign_pressure += 5;
Q.fiscal_stress += 3;
Q.public_mood_pending_rule_of_law_support += -6;
Q.public_mood_pending_rule_of_law_salience += 8;
Q.public_mood_pending_national_security_support += -5;
Q.news_headline =
    "Cold Western relations cut growth by " + drag +
    " points and raise unemployment by " + jobs + " points";
!}

= [+ date_label +] — Isolation acquires a price

The warnings stopped being diplomatic adjectives some months ago. A cohesion
payment that used to take six weeks now takes five months and comes back twice
for clarification. Polish borrowing carries a spread over Czech borrowing that
no ministry can explain in economic terms, because the reason is not economic.
An American manufacturer that spent two years choosing between Wrocław and
Ostrava has stopped returning the voivodeship's calls.

None of it is dramatic. There is no sanction, no expulsion, no headline —
only a country that has become slightly more expensive and slightly less
predictable to deal with, compounding quietly through every quarter.

The cost is already in the accounts, in growth that did not happen and jobs
that were placed elsewhere. No party statement reverses that. A government can
begin repairing the relationships or spend money shielding the people hit by
them; an opposition can prove what the isolation cost and publish the route
back.

- @west_repair
- @west_shield
- @west_double_down

@west_repair
title: [? if not left_in_government: Publish the cost of isolation and build a democratic route back?][? if left_in_government: Open an EU–US repair package with published benchmarks?]
subtitle: Opposition restores credibility; government can also recover part of the lost activity.
on-arrival: {!
const executive = Q.left_in_government && Q.government_has_confidence &&
    !Q.caretaker_government;
Q.eu_institutional_trust += executive ? 6 : 4;
Q.us_alliance_reliability += executive ? 5 : 3;
Q.constitutional_restraint += 3;
Q.foreign_pressure -= executive ? 4 : 2;
if (executive) {
    Q.economic_growth += 0.2;
    Q.government_delivery += 3;
    Q.foreign_policy_responsibility += 4;
    Q.foreign_authority_last_outcome =
        "Cabinet begins an institutional repair package and recovers part of the loss";
} else {
    Q.media_capacity += 3;
    Q.last_policy_stage = "Opposition foreign-economic audit";
    Q.last_policy_authority = "Parliamentary and European democratic channels";
    Q.last_policy_implementation = 0;
    Q.foreign_authority_last_outcome =
        "Opposition proves the cost of isolation and publishes a route back";
}
Q.news_headline = Q.foreign_authority_last_outcome;
!}

[? if left_in_government: The package is deliberately unromantic: a published
list of the agreements to be restored, the officials responsible for each, and
the dates by which the Commission and the State Department can expect an
answer. Benchmarks are easier to attack than a slogan and much harder to
abandon quietly.?][? if not left_in_government: We publish what the isolation
actually cost: the delayed payments with their dates, the spread against Czech
borrowing, the investment decisions that went elsewhere and the towns that were
bidding for them. Then we publish what reversing it would require.?]

[? if left_in_government: Two of the payment queues begin moving within the
quarter. The exporters notice before the voters do, which is the usual order of
these things and no comfort at all to a party that needs the voters.?][? if not
left_in_government: The government can ignore an opposition document. The
chambers of commerce cannot, and neither can the mayors whose industrial parks
are named in it. We have no power to restore a single agreement and we have made
the price of not restoring them a matter of public record.?]

- @poland_event_queue: Open the repair file

@west_shield
title: [? if not left_in_government: Table a worker-and-exporter shield for the cabinet to fund?][? if left_in_government: Fund a temporary worker-and-exporter shield?]
subtitle: Protects people from part of the shock; it does not repair the relationships that caused it.
choose-if: left_in_government = 0 or budget >= 1
unavailable-subtitle: A governing party needs one budget point to fund the shield.
on-arrival: {!
const executive = Q.left_in_government && Q.government_has_confidence &&
    !Q.caretaker_government;
if (executive && Q.budget >= 1) {
    Q.budget -= 1;
    Q.household_security += 3;
    Q.government_delivery += 3;
    Q.fiscal_stress += 2;
    Q.foreign_policy_responsibility += 2;
    Q.foreign_authority_last_outcome =
        "Government funds a temporary shield while Western access remains weak";
} else {
    Q.labor_credibility += 3;
    Q.household_policy_pressure += 3;
    Q.budget_promises += 1;
    Q.last_policy_stage = "Opposition economic shield proposal";
    Q.last_policy_authority = "Lewica parliamentary caucus";
    Q.last_policy_implementation = 0;
    Q.foreign_authority_last_outcome =
        "Opposition tables a shield that the cabinet must still fund";
}
Q.news_headline = Q.foreign_authority_last_outcome;
!}

[? if left_in_government: The money goes to the people the isolation reached
first: short-time working support in the export plants, and a bridge for the
suppliers around them who have no treasury of their own. It is the correct
use of a shield and it treats a symptom.?][? if not left_in_government: We table
the shield with the costings attached and dare the cabinet to explain why the
workers in Legnica and Gliwice should absorb a diplomatic decision they had no
part in.?]

[? if left_in_government: The Finance Ministry says so at the cabinet table, at
some length. Nothing in the package repairs a single relationship, and the bill
arrives every quarter that the relationships stay unrepaired.?][? if not
left_in_government: It is a proposal, not a payment. The government owns the
treasury and can leave our bill on the table indefinitely — but from today it has
to leave it there in public, beside the names of the plants running short
weeks.?]

- @poland_event_queue: Fund the shield

@west_double_down
title: Call the economic loss the price of sovereignty
subtitle: Mobilises confrontation and makes the next negotiation—and the next investment decision—harder.
on-arrival: party_unity += 2; pis_relation += 4; eu_institutional_trust -= 5; us_alliance_reliability -= 3; economic_growth -= 0.2 if left_in_government = 1; foreign_pressure += 4; far_right_agenda += 3; foreign_policy_responsibility += 4 if left_in_government = 1; foreign_authority_last_outcome = "Lewica accepts deeper isolation as the price of sovereignty"; news_headline = foreign_authority_last_outcome

We say what PiS has been saying and say it from the left: that a country which
can be disciplined through its payment queues was never sovereign, and that the
price of refusing is one worth paying. The line is genuinely popular in the
places that have already lost the investment.

It also removes our last argument for repair. The next negotiation opens with
Warsaw on record as indifferent to the outcome, the next investment committee
reads the same quote, and Konfederacja — which has held this position for
years without the social clause — asks the obvious question about who is
copying whom.

- @poland_event_queue: Say it out loud

@hungary_election_2022
# prose-audit: Grade A — The real 135–57 count and the warning about an alliance held together only by opposition are both exact, and each response now lands: contacts placed where the count says they are needed, a state channel Budapest gets to caption, or a celebration that costs the party its February argument.
title: Orbán wins another two-thirds majority
tags: poland_event, poland_event_calendar
priority: 32
order: 73
subtitle: Hungary gives PiS an ally and the Polish opposition a warning
face-image: img/poland/events/viktor-orban-2022.webp
new-page: true
max-visits: 1
view-if: continuous_campaign = 1 and year = 2022 and month = 4 and war_economy_2022_done = 1 and hungary_election_2022_done = 0
on-arrival: hungary_election_2022_done = 1; hungary_election_2022_result = "Fidesz–KDNP 135 seats · United for Hungary 57"; hungary_fidesz_in_power = 1; hungary_government = "Viktor Orbán — Fidesz–KDNP"; hungary_fidesz_seats = 135; hungary_relation += 2; hungary_fidesz_channel += 4; foreign_pressure += 2; news_headline = "Fidesz wins 135 of 199 seats and keeps its two-thirds majority"

= 3 April 2022 — Fidesz wins 135 seats

**Fidesz–KDNP wins 135 of 199 seats; the six-party United for Hungary list wins
57.** Viktor Orbán retains the two-thirds majority needed to shape the state
without opposition consent.

PiS presents the result as proof that confrontation with EU institutions can
be electorally rewarded. For Lewica and the rest of the Polish opposition,
Hungary is also a lesson in the weakness of a democratic alliance held
together only by opposition to one governing party.

- @hungary_2022_democrats
- @hungary_2022_pragmatic
- @hungary_2022_fidesz

@hungary_2022_democrats
title: Build durable ties to Hungarian democratic parties, media and monitors
subtitle: Strengthens the opposition case without pretending Warsaw can choose Hungary's government.
on-arrival: hungary_democratic_network += 7; eu_institutional_trust += 2; constitutional_restraint += 2; media_capacity += 1; news_headline = "The Polish opposition builds a Hungarian democratic channel beyond one failed list"

We put our contacts where the count says they are needed: the independent
outlets that survived, the monitoring organisations that documented the media
imbalance, and the municipal politicians who are now the opposition's only
executive experience.

None of it will win a Hungarian election, and we say so. What it does is ensure
that the next Hungarian opposition need not be introduced to the Polish left
from the beginning — and that somebody has written down the lesson of a
six-party alliance held together by nothing except Orbán.

- @poland_event_queue: Continue

@hungary_2022_pragmatic
title: Keep a narrow state and parliamentary channel open
subtitle: Practical access improves; Fidesz gains another claim to normalisation.
on-arrival: hungary_relation += 5; hungary_fidesz_channel += 2; eu_institutional_trust -= 1; ukraine_solidarity -= 1; news_headline = "Warsaw keeps Budapest businesslike after Orbán's victory"

We keep talking to the Hungarian state about the things states must discuss —
the crossings, the interconnector, the Poles working in Győr — and say nothing
about the government attached to it.

It is defensible and it is useful. It also appears in every Fidesz readout as
evidence that the Polish left regards Orbán as an ordinary partner. We supply
the meeting; Budapest writes the caption.

- @poland_event_queue: Continue

@hungary_2022_fidesz
title: Join PiS in celebrating the conservative axis
subtitle: PiS relations and Budapest access rise; democratic and Ukraine credibility fall.
on-arrival: pis_relation += 6; hungary_relation += 5; hungary_fidesz_channel += 7; eu_institutional_trust -= 4; ukraine_solidarity -= 3; progressives_dissent += 4; news_headline = "Lewica steps into the PiS–Fidesz axis"

Our speakers call the result proof that a sovereign social conservatism can
win, and decline to specify which parts of Orbán's twelve years they have in
mind. PiS is delighted to be agreed with. Budapest opens a door.

Ukrainian diplomats in Warsaw draw the obvious conclusion about a party
celebrating the one EU government obstructing arms transit. Inside Lewica, the
people who spent February arguing for solidarity find out what the argument was
worth.

- @poland_event_queue: Continue

