= 12 April 2026 — [+ hungary_election_2026_result +]

Hungarians decided this, and it is worth saying plainly in Warsaw, where every
party is about to explain the result as a verdict on itself. Polish contacts,
European party networks and years of cultivated relationships move a Hungarian
margin at the edges. They do not choose a Hungarian government.

[? if hungary_fidesz_in_power = 0: Péter Magyar's Tisza wins **141 seats**.
Fidesz–KDNP falls to **52**, Mi Hazánk takes **6**, and sixteen years of Orbán
government end. Polish politicians who treated Fidesz as Hungary itself must
now decide whether their relationship was with a country or a party.?]
[? if hungary_fidesz_in_power = 1: Orbán survives with **112 seats** against
Tisza's **81** and Mi Hazánk's **6**. The result is a counterfactual survival,
not a return to invulnerability: Hungary now has a single opposition force
capable of contesting power.?]

- @hungary_2026_change
- @hungary_2026_resistance
- @hungary_2026_state

@hungary_2026_change
title: Turn the democratic network into a Polish–Hungarian reset
subtitle: Available if Tisza forms government; opposition gains access, while cabinet can negotiate a state reset.
view-if: hungary_fidesz_in_power = 0
on-arrival: {!
const executive = Q.left_in_government && Q.government_has_confidence &&
    !Q.caretaker_government;
Q.hungary_relation += executive ? 8 : 5;
Q.eu_institutional_trust += executive ? 5 : 3;
Q.ukraine_solidarity += 3;
if (executive) {
    Q.government_delivery += 2;
    Q.foreign_policy_responsibility += 3;
    Q.foreign_authority_last_outcome =
        "Cabinet opens a democratic state reset with the Tisza government";
} else {
    Q.media_capacity += 2;
    Q.foreign_authority_last_outcome =
        "Years of opposition contact become access to Hungary's new government";
}
Q.news_headline = Q.foreign_authority_last_outcome;
!}

Sixteen years end in an afternoon, and the people who spent them building an
opposition out of nothing are suddenly a government with civil servants to
appoint. Our contacts stop being a solidarity gesture and start being a
diplomatic asset.

[? if left_in_government: Warsaw and Budapest can reopen files that were frozen
by two governments that preferred the quarrel to the interconnector.?][? if not
left_in_government: We hold relationships the Polish cabinet does not, which is
useful precisely as long as we are honest that we cannot conduct foreign policy
with them.?] Tisza will disappoint somebody within a year; every new government
does.

- @poland_event_queue: Reopen the files

@hungary_2026_resistance
title: Keep Hungarian democrats connected after Fidesz survives
subtitle: Available after an Orbán victory; builds the next contest without inventing Polish control.
view-if: hungary_fidesz_in_power = 1
on-arrival: hungary_democratic_network += 7; eu_institutional_trust += 2; constitutional_restraint += 2; media_capacity += 2 if left_in_government = 0; government_coalition_dissent += 2 if left_in_government = 1; foreign_authority_last_outcome = "Polish democratic channels remain open after Fidesz survives"; news_headline = foreign_authority_last_outcome

Orbán survives, and the useful thing to notice is what survived with him: a
single opposition force that held together, took a real share of the vote and
did not dissolve on the night. That is a different Hungary from 2022 even
though the government is the same.

We keep the channels to it open through the years in which nothing appears to
be happening. That is the whole of the work. It produces no announcement and it
is the only reason the relationship will exist when the next contest arrives.

- @poland_event_queue: Keep the channels open

@hungary_2026_state
title: Recognise the result and keep bilateral business separate from party loyalty
subtitle: A state relationship with Budapest, whether its government is Tisza or Fidesz.
on-arrival: hungary_relation += 5; constitutional_restraint += 2; foreign_policy_responsibility += 2 if left_in_government = 1; foreign_authority_last_outcome = "Warsaw recognises Hungary's result without treating one party as the country"; news_headline = foreign_authority_last_outcome

We deal with whoever holds the Hungarian state, on the border, the energy
connection and the Polish citizens working there, and we keep that entirely
separate from which Hungarian party we would prefer to see govern.

Both halves of that annoy somebody. Our own internationalists dislike the
correctness toward a government they campaigned against; Budapest dislikes
being told the correctness is procedural. It is, however, the only Hungarian
policy that survives a change of government in either capital.

- @poland_event_queue: Deal with the state

@us_review_2026
# prose-audit: Grade A — President, Prime Minister, Palace and portfolio are now placed in one live negotiation; every office-dependent route and all three alternatives have authored, constitutionally bounded aftermaths.
title: Washington reviews the Polish channel
subtitle: President [+ us_president +] wants one Polish answer from institutions that do not share one master
