= February 2026 — No alliance runs on autopilot

[? if us_administration = "Democratic": President **Kamala Harris** sends an
interagency delegation asking for three things before her first Warsaw visit:
a common Ukraine line, judicial repair that will survive the next election,
and an end to contradictory messages from cabinet, Palace and Sejm. KO calls
that an institutional partnership; PiS calls it American supervision.?]
[? if us_administration != "Democratic": President **[+ us_president +]** sends
an interagency delegation with a cooler manner and the same America First
arithmetic: higher defence spending, procurement alignment and privileged
access for American technology. Without Trump's personal Polish friendships,
every concession must now be purchased rather than photographed.?]

The visitors meet Prime Minister **[+ prime_minister +]**, Foreign Minister
**[+ foreign_minister +]** and President **[+ president_name +]** separately.
Each office gives a defensible answer; taken together, they sound like three
Polands. Lewica's own power determines whether it can merely expose that
confusion or must accept responsibility for ending it.

[? if foreign_authority_role = "ordinary_opposition": We hold no ministry and
cannot pledge the state. Our instruments are the Sejm committees, a published
doctrine and the party relationships we can persuade Washington to take
seriously.?]
[? if foreign_authority_role = "confidence_opposition": The cabinet survives
with our support but does not take our orders. We can attach reporting and
coordination conditions to future confidence, then make the government own
their execution.?]
[? if foreign_authority_role = "junior_no_mfa": Lewica sits in coalition but
another party holds the diplomatic pen. We can force the question into
coalition council, trade support and dissent in public; we cannot sign for the
Foreign Minister.?]
[? if foreign_authority_role = "junior_mfa": Foreign Minister **[+
foreign_minister +]** is ours, but Prime Minister **[+ prime_minister +]** sets
the cabinet mandate. A ministry initiative can build the compact or be
overruled before Washington.?]
[? if foreign_authority_role = "lead_no_mfa": Prime Minister **[+
prime_minister +]** can set cabinet strategy, but a coalition partner controls
Foreign Affairs. Direction is possible; obedience and coalition peace are not
the same thing.?]
[? if foreign_authority_role = "lead_mfa": Lewica controls both the
Chancellery and Foreign Affairs. Nobody else can be blamed for a promise that
fails or an alliance allowed to drift.?]
[? if foreign_authority_role = "caretaker": A caretaker can keep telephones
answered and signed commitments alive. It cannot bind the cabinet that comes
next simply to make a difficult meeting look successful.?]

- @review_compact
- @review_parliament
- @review_europe
- @review_presidential

@review_compact
title: [? if foreign_authority_role = "ordinary_opposition": Publish a cabinet–Palace compact and force both offices to answer it?][? if foreign_authority_role = "confidence_opposition": Make a cabinet–Palace compact the price of continued support?][? if foreign_authority_role = "junior_no_mfa": Take the compact to coalition council and force the portfolio owner to answer?][? if foreign_authority_role = "junior_mfa": Negotiate the ministry's side without claiming the Prime Minister's mandate?][? if foreign_authority_role = "lead_no_mfa": Set the cabinet line and make the coalition Foreign Minister carry it?][? if foreign_authority_role = "lead_mfa": Put the cabinet and Foreign Ministry behind one Washington compact?][? if foreign_authority_role = "caretaker": Keep the channels open and leave no trap for the next cabinet?]
subtitle: Give Washington one account of the alliance without pretending the Palace belongs to the cabinet.
